    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We present petrophysical data of host and fault rock samples from aeolian Aztec sandstone, Valley of Fire State Park, Nevada, that has been deformed by strike-slip faults formed by progressive shearing along joint zones. The data include bulk mineralogy, porosity, permeability, and mercury-injection capillary pressure measurements. To investigate the impact of increasing slip on fault zone characteristics, three sample localities with slip magni- tudes of 30 m, 60 m, and 160 m were investigated. No bulk mineralogical changes between host and fault rock have been detected. Fault rock permeability is signifi- cantly lower than mean host rock permeability, showing a one to three order of magnitude reduction. Changes in fault rock porosity ranged from a factor of four decrease to a factor of two increase. The increase in porosity was only observed in two samples and is not considered typi- cal of the fault rock. From limited data, the entry pres- sure of fault rock is one to two orders of magnitude higher than its host rock equivalent. For standard oil and brine properties, this increase in entry pressure in the fault zone corresponds to a sealing capacity of up to 112 m of oil column, corresponding to a class C seal in the Sneider classification system (Sneider et al., 1997). These petrophysical data show that faults in high permeability sand-prone systems will act as significant barriers to fluid flow during reservoir production and may be capable of sealing large hydrocarbon columns on an exploration time-scale as well.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
